    {
      "paragraph": 5,
      "index": 2,
      "fill": "if they remain in their purity",
      "notes": "Virginals correspond to the light that never entered mixture; their defining predicate is remaining untouched/unstained. The clause sets the condition for the subsequent ‘even if they go out against the enemy… they do not taste death’.",
      "confidence": "moderate"
    },
    {
      "paragraph": 5,
      "index": 3,
      "fill": "emy’s war ... they do not",
      "notes": "¶3 already defined virginal as light that did not ‘taste death’ and did not ‘come to the enemy’s war’. Here the structure reverses: even if they go out against the enemy’s war, they still do not taste death—because their state is not mixture but guarded purity.",
      "confidence": "moderate"
    },
